      <floats-group id="article-131884.s16">
        <fig id="article-131884.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Palatal Cysts of the Newborn. Small white-to-yellow nodules appear along the median palatal raphe or junction of the hard and soft palate, typically seen in neonates. These cysts are benign, keratin-filled, and tend to resolve spontaneously without intervention. Contributed by H Olmo, DDS, MS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="PCNb" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-131884.s17">
        <fig id="article-131884.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Epidermoid Cyst on Lateral Tongue.&#x000a0;The image shows a well-defined, smooth, soft tissue swelling located on the lateral border of the tongue. This benign cyst originates from epidermal inclusions and may enlarge slowly, occasionally causing functional disturbance depending on size and location. Contributed by Heather Olmo, DDS, MS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="01-028" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-131884.s18">
        <fig id="article-131884.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Lymphoepithelial Cysts on the Posterior Pharyngeal Pillar. Multiple small, smooth, pale-yellowish nodules located on and around the posterior pharyngeal pillars, consistent with lymphoepithelial cysts associated with lymphoid tissue aggregates in the Waldeyer ring. Contributed by Heather Olmo, DDS, MS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="LE__Cyst__and__aggregate__01-028" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-131884.s19">
        <fig id="article-131884.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Nasopalatine Duct Cyst on Radiography. This image of the anterior maxilla displays a well-defined, radiolucent lesion located at the midline, between the roots of the central incisors. This finding is characteristic of a nasopalatine duct cyst. Contributed by Heather Olmo, DDS, MS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Nasopalatine__duct__cyst__-__2" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-131884.s20">
        <fig id="article-131884.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Nasopalatine Duct Cyst. A round, firm swelling is&#x000a0;present in the anterior midline of the palate, consistent with a nasopalatine duct cyst. The hard palate and maxillary dentition are also shown. Contributed by Heather Olmo, DDS, MS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="01-078" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
